WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.252 --> 00:00:06.829
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.829 --> 00:00:13.083
That is an impala, or
as we call it in South Africa a springbok,

00:00:13.083 --> 00:00:18.284
I shot one dead when I was
ten years old and I hated it.

00:00:19.682 --> 00:00:23.580
I hated seeing such a beautiful
wild animal die because of me.

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:28.450
Hunting clearly wasn't for me, so
my first hunt was also my last.

00:00:29.530 --> 00:00:34.111
Since then Africa's majestic
wildlife has become of almost

00:00:34.111 --> 00:00:36.362
spiritual importance to me.

00:00:36.362 --> 00:00:41.097
There's something quite liberating about
looking into the eyes of an animal that

00:00:41.097 --> 00:00:42.068
could kill you.

00:00:42.068 --> 00:00:46.330
And it's humbling to see
an elephant family interact and

00:00:46.330 --> 00:00:50.870
think that perhaps it's us who
have much to learn from them.

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:54.639
So there is no cause I feel stronger
about than conservation, and

00:00:54.639 --> 00:00:59.791
then something happened that really upped
the stakes, Cecil the lion, remember that?

00:00:59.791 --> 00:01:04.324
A global news storm, a hunt gone wrong,
the wrong lion shot,

00:01:04.324 --> 00:01:08.174
a famous lion, Cecil,
star of BBC documentaries.

00:01:08.174 --> 00:01:13.666
The shooter, an American dentist
called Walter Palmer, the reaction.

00:01:13.666 --> 00:01:15.213
&gt;&gt; Walter fucking Palmer.

00:01:15.213 --> 00:01:17.784
&gt;&gt; How fucking demented
this mother fucker is.

00:01:17.784 --> 00:01:20.487
&gt;&gt; May you fucking burn to death.

00:01:20.487 --> 00:01:23.177
&gt;&gt; I want somebody to
take fucking revenge.

00:01:23.177 --> 00:01:26.420
&gt;&gt; What would happen [INAUDIBLE].

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:29.064
&gt;&gt; Sick son of a bitch.

00:01:29.064 --> 00:01:33.571
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; I know, that was ugly, but

00:01:33.571 --> 00:01:38.415
welcome to the divisive
world of trophy hunting.

00:01:38.415 --> 00:01:41.805
Every year thousands of foreigners
go on organized hunts in Africa,

00:01:41.805 --> 00:01:46.220
they take home with them the animal
head or trophy to hang on their wall.

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:48.690
They pay tens or
even hundreds of thousands of dollars for

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:51.860
these trips, the costs include a fee for
the animal shot,

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:57.428
as well as professional hunting guides,
trackers, accommodation, and travel costs.

00:01:57.428 --> 00:02:03.650
Now it's easy to hate this, but what
if I told you that hunting can be good,

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:09.206
that it can help protect Africa's
wildlife, preserving scenes like this.

00:02:09.206 --> 00:02:12.833
I know, counterintuitive right,
and today I'm not going to

00:02:12.833 --> 00:02:16.698
convince you not to hate photos
like this one if you already do.

00:02:16.698 --> 00:02:20.789
And I'm also not going to get into
a debate about the ethics of killing

00:02:20.789 --> 00:02:23.650
animals, whether they be lions or
chickens.

00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:27.033
Emotionally, I wish we
didn't need hunting, but

00:02:27.033 --> 00:02:31.438
objectively right now regulated
trophy hunting is a good thing for

00:02:31.438 --> 00:02:35.072
Africa, there are three reasons why,
let's start.

00:02:35.072 --> 00:02:39.470
First, resources,
conservation doesn't happen by accident,

00:02:39.470 --> 00:02:44.080
it's an intentional,
managed process requiring resources.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:48.091
Huge areas of land, fences, water
supplies, managers and ecologists, and

00:02:48.091 --> 00:02:51.720
squads of armed rangers to
protect against illegal poaching,

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:56.110
this all costs money in a part of
the world where people are very poor.

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:01.140
It's hard to expect people who live like
this to care about nature conservation

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:07.250
before their basic human needs have been
met, food, shelter, financial security.

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:10.950
Which brings us to the second point,
the biggest killer of wildlife by far,

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:13.073
anywhere in the world,
is human encroachment.

00:03:13.073 --> 00:03:18.084
Wild habitats are destroyed to create
farmland, animals are hunted for meat,

00:03:18.084 --> 00:03:23.264
remember in Africa many people eat protein
starved diets and cannot afford meat.

00:03:23.264 --> 00:03:26.721
Farmers in America might complain
about the occasional wolf,

00:03:26.721 --> 00:03:31.167
well try being a farmer in Africa,
hyenas, leopards, pythons, crocodiles.

00:03:31.167 --> 00:03:33.045
Elephants are shot because
they trample crops,

00:03:33.045 --> 00:03:34.707
lions killed because they eat livestock.

00:03:34.707 --> 00:03:37.723
Hippos offer danger when they're alive,
but

00:03:37.723 --> 00:03:40.530
can feed a whole village if they're dead.

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:45.690
And this problem is only getting much
bigger, Africa's population is exploding,

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:50.060
look at this chart, epic performance
from Africa over the next 100 years.

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:57.540
So there has to be an economic
incentive to conserve wildlife,

00:03:57.540 --> 00:03:58.616
and hunting can provide it.

00:03:58.616 --> 00:04:03.280
By setting hunting quotas below
the animal population growth rate and

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:06.280
by targeting older male animals,

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:11.120
hunting can be both sustainable and
profitable and that gives landowners

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:14.659
an incentive to convert from
commercial farming to game reserves.

00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:19.600
It works, since regulated rhino hunting
was introduced in South Africa,

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:23.080
the white rhino population
has grown tenfold.

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:27.264
The best example is Namibia's Community
Conservancy Program, Namibia's gone and

00:04:27.264 --> 00:04:32.230
given ownership and management of
wild land over to local communities.

00:04:32.230 --> 00:04:33.840
Income from hunting and

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:38.850
tourism goes directly into conservation
expenses, local communities gain income,

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:43.280
jobs, meat, these conservancies now
cover an area larger than England.

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:49.970
Okay, so then what about the other option
for generating income, photo tourism?

00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:54.850
I long for the day when tourism
can carry the weight of sustaining

00:04:54.850 --> 00:04:58.620
Africa's wild areas but
we're a long way off from that.

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:03.110
Tourism is a large industry but
it's concentrated in a few areas,

00:05:03.110 --> 00:05:07.680
it needs to be safe and
close to airports, whereas trophy hunters

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:11.920
will go anywhere and
pay top dollar to pursue their passion.

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:16.350
Let's look again at those Namibian
conservancies, they're clustered up in

00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:19.080
the north of the country and on this map
they've been color coded to show which of

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:22.460
them are currently profitable, green,
and which are loss making, red.

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:28.620
Now let's simulate what would happen
if we removed the benefits of hunting,

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:32.710
the map changes green to red.

00:05:32.710 --> 00:05:38.230
And those red areas, they'd go back to
what they were 30 years ago, cattle farms,

00:05:38.230 --> 00:05:41.830
land over-grazed to dust, so you see,

00:05:41.830 --> 00:05:47.049
hunting is sustaining remote wild places
that many people have never heard of.

00:05:47.049 --> 00:05:51.440
And now, now hunting can help pay for

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:56.480
protection against a new crisis,
the rampant illegal wildlife trade

00:05:56.480 --> 00:06:01.450
driven by demand from China and Vietnam
for rhino horn, ivory, and lion bone.

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:08.190
Rhino horn is now worth more
than cocaine or gold, and

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:11.500
that means these incredible animals

00:06:12.910 --> 00:06:16.840
having their faces hacked off,

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:21.552
sometimes while they're still alive, and

00:06:21.552 --> 00:06:27.060
this must never be okay,
we have to be better than this.

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:30.305
Unlike hunting, this killing is
unregulated, unsustainable, and

00:06:30.305 --> 00:06:34.324
totally out of control, and we need every
conservation tool available to prevent it.

00:06:36.296 --> 00:06:41.338
Now not all hunting is good,
as in any system there are problems and

00:06:41.338 --> 00:06:44.770
abuses, and
canned hunting is one of those.

00:06:45.970 --> 00:06:51.310
Animals, often lions, bred in captivity so
that they can be shot in staged hunts,

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:54.710
these lions are tame,
they can't even hunt for themselves.

00:06:54.710 --> 00:06:57.250
They're taken away from their mothers
when they're three days old so

00:06:57.250 --> 00:06:59.450
that the mothers will breed again and

00:06:59.450 --> 00:07:03.940
again, real lions are raised by their
mothers for at least 18 months.

00:07:05.170 --> 00:07:06.940
Hunters need to take responsibility for

00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:09.540
stopping this because it's their
dollars that keep it going.

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:13.806
Other hunting systems may be run
with incompetence or corruption,

00:07:13.806 --> 00:07:16.105
meaning the benefits don't
flow to local people.

00:07:16.105 --> 00:07:18.642
But this means we need
to seek improvement,

00:07:18.642 --> 00:07:22.012
not shut the whole thing down,
and right now this is real.

00:07:22.012 --> 00:07:25.428
Since Cecil the lion, 45 airlines
have banned the transport of animal

00:07:25.428 --> 00:07:28.900
trophies and several Western governments
are considering import bans,

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:30.470
the whole thing is under threat.

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:35.450
And as we saw in Namibia, a knee-jerk,

00:07:35.450 --> 00:07:38.700
uninformed ban could lead to
the loss of huge areas of wild land.

00:07:40.150 --> 00:07:45.070
And then, what about the 184,000 people

00:07:45.070 --> 00:07:48.650
who live on Namibia's conservancies,
who's thinking of them?

00:07:49.940 --> 00:07:52.970
What about African citizens of
other countries who live with and

00:07:52.970 --> 00:07:54.170
support trophy hunting?

00:07:55.250 --> 00:07:58.000
Almost all the anti-hunting
backlash comes from wealthy people.

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:04.270
It's so ironic, it sometimes feels
like the two things that Facebook

00:08:04.270 --> 00:08:09.120
warriors around the world care about when
it comes to Africa are human poverty and

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:09.971
wild animals, yet

00:08:09.971 --> 00:08:15.250
the anti-hunting campaign could hurt
both human poverty and wild animals.

00:08:17.030 --> 00:08:21.990
So I dislike hunting, but

00:08:21.990 --> 00:08:26.160
the majesty of Africa's wildlife has
been one of the highlights of my life,

00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:29.589
I must do what I can to preserve it for
future generations.

00:08:30.630 --> 00:08:34.200
That means making hard and
informed choices and

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:38.870
it may also mean having to cooperate and
coexist with those who are different from

00:08:38.870 --> 00:08:42.400
me, those whom I may never understand,
in pursuit of a shared goal.

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:45.428
I hope that this talk has
helped you to see some of

00:08:45.428 --> 00:08:49.684
the complexities of conservation and
how hunting can play a role.

00:08:49.684 --> 00:08:54.962
And finally, I really hope that
everyone in this room one day has

00:08:54.962 --> 00:09:00.044
the chance to come to Africa,
have the holiday of a lifetime,

00:09:00.044 --> 00:09:05.547
live to the rhythm of nature,
shoot with a camera, not with a gun.

00:09:05.547 --> 00:09:09.301
You will support jobs and
conservation, and

00:09:09.301 --> 00:09:14.654
if enough of you do that one day
we won't need to kill to protect.

00:09:14.654 --> 00:09:16.261
Thank you.

00:09:16.261 --> 00:09:23.998
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:23.998 --> 00:09:28.156
[MUSIC]

